Thank you for your reply, I guess that leaves me a little confused, here: https://groups.google.com/forum/#!topic/lavaan/0ZUwUmXmIq0 you seem to suggest that the standardized coefficient, 'std.all', is the semipartial correlation coefficient and squaring it is the proportion of variance uniquely attributable to that variable. In your response in this message, when you say "That is the case for the semipartial correlation, but not the standardized slope." I get the impression you are saying 'std.all', the standardized slope, is not the semipartial correlation coefficient or cannot be squared to get that r2 value.  So how do I get semipartial correlation coefficients that I can present as an r2? Thank you for your time in explaining this.

On Monday, May 14, 2018 at 10:51:22 PM UTC-5, Terrence Jorgensen wrote:
> > Yes, my understanding is that 'std.all' is the standardized coefficient or the  semipartial correlation coefficient
>
> Those 2 things are not generally the same, although in practice they are often very close.
>
> > and it's square is the unique portion of variance explained by that variable.
>
> That is the case for the semipartial correlation, but not the standardized slope.
>
